The code statements that lead to the bug are:
struct papr_hvpipe_hdr hdr;
hdr.version = 0;
hdr.flags = 0;
ret = copy_to_user(buf, &hdr, HVPIPE_HDR_LEN);
Reason:
Only `version` and `flags` fields of the stack-allocated `hdr` structure are explicitly initialized, leaving padding bytes (`reserved[3]` and `reserved2[40]`) uninitialized. `copy_to_user` then copies the entire structure (including these uninitialized bytes) to userspace, leaking kernel stack memory.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
struct papr_hvpipe_hdr hdr = {};
hdr.version = 0;
hdr.flags = 0;
ret = copy_to_user(buf, &hdr, HVPIPE_HDR_LEN);
Reason:
The declaration `hdr = {}` zero-initializes the entire structure, including all padding bytes. Even though `version` and `flags` are set afterwards, the zero-initialization ensures no uninitialized data remains, preventing the stack memory leak when `copy_to_user` copies the structure.

